-- title:
--   Other-orbit pole in the blown-up supersingular chart
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q\ge 5$ and $\ell\ge 3$ are primes with $\ell\neq q$ (hypotheses `hq`, `hℓ3`, `hℓq`), and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number divisible by neither $q$ (`hqM'`) nor $\ell$ (`hℓM'`). The field $L$ has characteristic $0$, $\xi\in L$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is pinned by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M'))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the subfield generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise image along $\mathbb Q\to L$ of the $q$-expansion function field of the modular curve $X_H$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$, where $H=$ `levelH (q*ℓ) M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   The ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, with an algebra structure on $L$ making $L$ its fraction field, and with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$; $q\in\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hξA`). The element $j\in K$ has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) (`hj`), the base change to $L$ of the $q$-expansion $T^{-1}+\dots$ of the modular $j$-invariant, and is nonzero. Further, $\varpi\in A$ generates $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,A$ (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$, $\mathfrak X=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) for the pushout of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the middle chart algebra along the two maps to $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ and to the spectrum of the chart algebra attached to $j^{-1}$, `toBase` for its structure morphism to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$, `XFin` $=\mathrm{Spec}\,C$, and `ιFin` for the chart morphism $\mathrm{XFin}\to\mathfrak X$. The ideal $y$ of $C$ is maximal (`hy`), contains the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi\colon C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $t\in\Omega$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $t$ has no nonzero point annihilated by $q$.
--
--   For $\gamma\in SL(2,\mathbb Z)$, the predicate `IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ` says of an $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ that it realises the slash action by the matrix `conjElemN (q*ℓ) γ⁻¹` on quotients of $q$-expansions: for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $p_g\neq 0$ over $\mathbb Q$, all $x\in K$ whose Laurent series is $p_f/p_g$, and all ring homomorphisms $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$, one has $\iota(\tau x)\cdot q\text{-exp}(g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)\gamma^{-1})=q\text{-exp}(f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)\gamma^{-1})$. Automorphisms so attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ are called below the attached automorphisms.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` is the uniform Drinfeld-chart input. It requires, for every point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$, every stalk element $\varpi_z$ equal to the germ at $z$ of the global function on $\mathfrak X$ coming from $\varpi$ through `toBase` and `Scheme.ΓSpecIso` and lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk, every point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin.base y' = z`, and under the same supersingularity condition at $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ as in `hss`, the existence of a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma\colon A\to W$ with $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W=(\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and an isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ onto $S=W[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v-fu)$, subject to seven clauses (summarised here), stated in terms of $S$, the quotient map $\mathrm{mkS}$, the completion map $\mathrm{toC}$, and the germ map $\mathrm{germY}\colon C\to\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ obtained by identifying $C$ with the sections of $\mathfrak X$ over the image of `ιFin` and taking germs at $z$: (i) $e$ carries the germ of $a\in A$ to the class of the constant $\sigma a$; (ii) every attached automorphism for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ maps $C$ into $C$; (iii) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')\cap\Gamma(\ell)$ the attached automorphism satisfies $\tau a-a\in y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ for all $a\in C$; (iv) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, an attached automorphism preserving $C$ and inducing the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c\in W$ and a $2\times2$ matrix $M$ over $W$ such that $\theta$ intertwines with $\tau$ through $e\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$, $\theta$ fixes constants, $\theta(X_j)\equiv\sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0,X_1$, $c^{q+1}\equiv1$ and $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$, $c\equiv1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, and $c\not\equiv1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (v) a branch-separation clause: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and prime ideals $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each omitting the class of $X_0$ or that of $X_1$, each containing the class of $\sigma\varpi$ and some $a_iX_0+b_iX_1+h_i$ with $h_i\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, with $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$, the contractions of $P_1,P_2$ along $e\circ\mathrm{toC}$ are distinct; (vi) an Igusa-valuation clause: for a prime $P$ of $S$ omitting the class of $X_0$ or that of $X_1$, containing the class of $\sigma\varpi$ and some $X_0+h$ with $h\in(X_0,X_1)^2$, and for $a\in C$, the element $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a)$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,A$; (vii) a Hasse-type clause: [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the substitution $T\mapsto T^{q\ell}$ in the $j$-expansion) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0\in A$ with $j_{q\ell}-a_0\in y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0\ge1$ and $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_{i\le e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b\in W$ not both in $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$ with $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$, such that $e$ sends the germ of $j_{q\ell}-a_0$ to the class of $h$.
--
--   There is then a fixed such configuration: a point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ with $\varpi_z$ as above (`hϖz`) in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), a point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin.base y' = z` (`hy'`), supersingular (`hss'`), and $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}=y$ (`hy'y`); and a fixed witness at $z$: a complete discrete valuation domain $W_1$, $\sigma_1\colon A\to W_1$ with $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W_1=(\sigma_1\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`), units $u_1,v_1$ and $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hu₁`, `hv₁`, `hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the adic completion of $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ onto $S=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v_1-f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this witness exactly the seven-clause conjunction (i)–(vii) above, instantiated at $z$, $y'$, $W_1$, $\sigma_1$, $f_1$, $u_1$, $v_1$, $e_1$.
--
--   The remaining data are: an ideal $J$ of $C$ pinned by `hJ` to be the infimum of the set of ideals of the form $\tau^{-1}(\mathfrak c)$, where $\mathfrak c$ is the contraction along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, and $\tau$ runs over the attached automorphisms for $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ that map $C$ into $C$; an $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$ pinned by `hB` to be the restriction of scalars to $A$ of the $C$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $\{x\in K:\ x\varpi_t\in J\}$, that is $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$; and a valuation subring $W$ of $K$ with $B\subseteq W$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hR1` gives $C\le B$ and that every $f\in K$ is of the form $g/h$ with $g,h\in B$, $h\neq0$; `hR2` gives that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` has five clauses: an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it comes from $A$, $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$, $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, an element of $C$ lies in $y$ exactly when it lies in $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$, and $W$ is the set of $f\in K$ of the form $g/h$ with $g,h\in B$ and $h\notin\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,W$. Finally `hRad` asserts the existence of $n\ge1$ such that for every $a\in C$ lying in $y$, the $n$-th power of $e_1(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a))$ lies in the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$.
--
--   The conclusion is a single statement about maximal ideals outside the orbit of $y$: for every ideal $y''$ of $C$ which is maximal and contains the image of $\varpi$, and which satisfies the non-orbit condition that for every $\gamma\in SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every attached automorphism $\tau$ for $\gamma^{-1}$ it fails that for all $b\in C$ with $\tau b\in C$ one has $b\in y$ if and only if $\tau b\in y''$, there exists an element $b$ of $K$ such that $b\in B$ and such that for every $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, every attached automorphism $\tau$ for $\gamma^{-1}$, and every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ with the two properties that every element of $C$ lies in $V$ and that every $c\in C$ with $\tau^{-1}(c)\in C$ and $\tau^{-1}(c)\in y''$ lies in $\mathrm{maximalIdeal}\,V$ (for any proof that $c\in V$), one has $b\notin V$. Thus the blow-up algebra $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$ contains a function with a pole at every valuation of $K$ dominating $C$ whose centre is a $\tau$-translate of such a $y''$.
--
--   This is the other-orbit pole property of the blown-up supersingular chart: the algebra $B=C[J/\varpi_t]$ obtained by blowing up the weighted centre $J$ at the supersingular point $y$ admits, for each supersingular maximal ideal over $\varpi$ outside the orbit of $y$ under the attached level automorphisms, an element with a pole at every valuation centred there. It is used in the assembly of the pole and Igusa-valuation properties of the blown-up chart in the construction of the auxiliary integral model at level $q\ell$.
